ption {:value "353461"} "Kayak Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "353482"} "Kayak Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "353492"} "Kayak Fours, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "353508"} "Canadian Singles, 200 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "353521"} "Canadian Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "353532"} "Canadian Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "353554"} "Kayak Singles, 200 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "353567"} "Kayak Singles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "353580"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "353590"} "Kayak Fours, 500 metres, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "307077"} "2012"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/990"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Kayak Doubles, 200 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "17 – 18 August 2016"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/912"} "Estádio da Lagoa, Lagoa Rodrigo de Freitas, Rio de Janeiro"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/353472"} "26 from 13 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "With limited consistency in the World and European Championship results in the last four years, and defending champion Russia not present for the event in Rio due to its doping scandal, the Men’s K-2 200, particularly given its short distance, was one of the most open contests in sprint canoeing. The reigning World Champions were Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/136538"} "Sándor Tótka"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/136537"} "Péter Molnár"] ", while their European equivalents were " [:a {:href "/athletes/135900"} "Nebojša Grujić"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/122424"} "Marko Novaković"] " of Serbia, who were also the 2014 World Champions, the 2015 European runners-up, and the 2015 World bronze medallists. One other duo of note was Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/134143"} "Tom Liebscher"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/90189"} "Ronald Rauhe"] ", who were the 2014 and 2015 European Champions, the 2014 World runners-up, and the 2016 European bronze medallists. Rauhe had a long history in the sport and Olympic medals that spanned back to bronze in the K-2 500 in 2000 and included gold in the K-2 500 in 2004."] "\n\n" [:p {} "None of these individuals, however, qualified directly for the finals. Lithuania, represented by 2014 and 2015 European bronze medallists " [:a {:href "/athletes/135043"} "Aurimas Lankas"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/135044"} "Edvinas Ramanauskas"] ", won the first opening heat while " [:a {:href "/athletes/133653"} "Cristian Toro"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/116612"} "Saúl Craviotto"] " of Spain took the second. Spain had not reached the World podium in the K-2 200 since 2011, but Craviotto was a decorated canoeist who was a 2008 Olympic Champion in the K2-500 and the 2012 runner-up in the K-1 200. Hungary then won the first semifinal, while Germany and Serbia placed second and third respectively behind " [:a {:href "/athletes/123211"} "Liam Heath"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/123214"} "Jon Schofield"] " of Great Britain, the 2012 Olympic bronze medallists and 2013 World runners-up. In the mad rush of the final, the Spanish duo came out in front to win gold convincingly, but careful analysis of the results was required to determine the placement of the next six positions, as only 0.331 seconds separated them all. In the end, Great Britain nabbed silver 0.014 seconds ahead of Lithuania, who in turn captured bronze by finishing 0.03 seconds in front of Hungary. Craviotto would add to his Olympic medals two days later by taking bronze in the K-1 200."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "94/150"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/116612"} "Saúl Craviotto"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/133653"} "Cristian Toro"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ESP"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ESP.png"}] "ESP"]] [:td {} "31.161 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "32.075 (1 h2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "99/152"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/123211"} "Liam Heath"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/123214"} "Jon Schofield"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "31.534 (3 h1)"] [:td {} "31.899 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "32.368 (2 h2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "157/158"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/135043"} "Aurimas Lankas"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/135044"} "Edvinas Ramanauskas"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/LTU"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/LTU.png"}] "LTU"]] [:td {} "31.755 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "32.382 (3 h2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "243/74"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/136538"} "Sándor Tótka"] " / " [:a {:href "/athlet